# .gitattributes — line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: this repo is developed on Windows. Any tool that writes a
# source file through a text-mode API (Python's write_text, PowerShell's
# Set-Content, some editors) silently converts LF to CRLF. Git then reports
# EVERY line of that file as changed — an 11-line edit to registry.js once
# showed up as 981 changed lines, which reads like a rogue reformat.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes that structurally impossible: whatever a tool
# writes, the blob stored in git is LF, and every checkout gets LF back.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Binary: never convert, never diff as text ─────────────────────────────
# text=auto already sniffs these correctly, but being explicit means a
# corrupted image can never be blamed on an eol filter.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary

# ── Shell scripts must be LF even on a Windows checkout ───────────────────
# A CRLF shebang line makes the script unrunnable. scripts/setup-hooks.sh
# runs from the postinstall hook, so this is load-bearing.
*.sh text eol=lf

# ── Generated data blobs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Verbatim upstream copies (grindinggear/skilltree-export, atlastree-export)
# and machine-generated data. They still normalize to LF — they're just not
# worth rendering as a line diff: swapping one atlas tree is a genuine 22,000
# line change that no human reads, and it drowns the actual code review.
#
# `-diff` shows "Binary files differ" instead. Remove these three lines if you
# ever need to inspect a tree change hunk-by-hunk.
src/data/passive/passiveTreeData_*.json -diff
src/data/atlas/atlasTreeData.json       -diff
src/data/timeless/*.json                -diff
